  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDry Canadian high pressure is pushing in from the north and will\nkeep rain associated with a low pressure system mostly south of\nour area. Clouds will start to clear across the northern CWA\nthis morning with persistent clouds and a few rain showers\nacross the southern CWA. Rainfall amounts continue to decrease,\nand only about 0.10 inches or lower is possible along the I-80 \ncorridor in Luzerne County. Elsewhere, measurable rainfall is \nnot expected, but can't rule out a few sprinkles early this\nmorning from the Southern Tier southward. \nHigh pressure and ridging will set up over the region later\ntoday and settle there through at least Friday. This setup \nbrings northeasterly flow, advecting dry and cool air into the \nforecast area. By later Thursday an upper level low phases \ntogether with northern stream energy to develop a 500mb low over\nVirginia, but again our area remains dry as ridging should hold.\n\nTemperatures will be in the 60s for highs each day, with lows \nin the mid to upper 30s to low 40s each night. There will be\npotential for patchy frost for at least northern Oneida County\nthe next few nights and an advisory may be needed if confidence\nin coverage increases. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nModel guidance continues to struggle with the complex weather \npattern setting up Friday into the weekend. As mentioned above some \nupper level energy will dive south across New England Thursday and \nmerge with the 500mb low drifting east from the Ohio Valley into the \nMid-Atlantic. 0z GFS and ECMWF keep most impacts from this\nsystem well east of our area, but some wraparound moisture could\nbring some rain showers Friday and this weekend. It will take a\nmajor shift in the track to impact our area, and appears more of\nan issue for Long Island and New England at this time. There is\npotential that high pressure holds all the way through the week\nand the weekend, with dry conditions. \n\nTrends in the latest guidance will continue to be monitored \nclosely in the coming days just in case the storms track shifts. At\nthis time, the possible outcomes range from mostly sunny and \ndry conditions Friday/Saturday...to cooler and cloudy with \nperiods of rain, especially for our far eastern Catskill zones.\nNorth- northwest winds could also be breezy and gusty, 8-15 mph\nwith gusts up to 20-25 mph in this timeframe.",
